# Shell scripts must stay LF: they may run from a Windows working copy under
# WSL or Git Bash, where a CRLF shebang/line ending breaks bash outright.
# (The Git-LFS rules that used to live here are gone — the model blobs moved
# to assets on the `models-v1` GitHub release; see scripts/fetch-models.ps1.)
*.sh text eol=lf

# The detection bundle and its smoke corpus are BYTE-EXACT artifacts, and two
# separate things depend on that:
#   * the corpus encodes zero-width (U+200B) and NBSP (U+00A0) separators on
#     purpose — they are the payload, not formatting;
#   * the .yar files' SHA-256s are published in the update manifest, so a
#     checkout that rewrote line endings would produce a bundle whose digests
#     no longer match what raw.githubusercontent.com serves.
# Line-ending translation would silently break both. Keep them LF everywhere.
detection/** text eol=lf

# V35 Phase M: the generated-plugin TEMPLATE. `harness::opencode::plugin`
# asserts render(template, flags) equals each golden byte for byte, and the
# rendered text is written into a project's `.opencode/plugin/`. Neither should
# depend on how a contributor's Git checked the template out, so both stay LF
# everywhere — same reasoning as the detection bundle above, one layer up.
src-tauri/src/harness/opencode/templates/plugin.js text eol=lf

# The whole harness fixture tree is BYTE-EXACT, and every kind of file under it
# is compared or embedded verbatim by a test:
#   * `<id>/goldens/plugin.*.js` — the pre-Phase-M plugin goldens, asserted
#     against the rendered template (they lived under fixtures/plugin-goldens/
#     until V40 Phase G moved them here, which is how they lost their rule and
#     started failing the Windows runner's `core.autocrlf=true` checkout);
#   * `registry.json` — written by `harness::info`'s parity test and imported by
#     vitest, so a CRLF checkout made the Rust test rewrite it and fail;
#   * `<id>/<version>/tui/**` — the Claude TUI rendered-tail scrapes.
#     `processing::permission` `include_str!`s them and feeds them to the
#     detector unchanged, and `permission_body()` slices one by its exact footer
#     suffix, so translated line endings change the matched bytes themselves;
#   * `<id>/<version>/*.jsonl` / `*.json` — canary corpora, `include_str!`d and
#     compared against the committed file by `harness::canary`;
#   * `MANIFEST.toml` — parsed beside each corpus.
# One rule for the tree, so a fixture added tomorrow inherits it instead of
# reintroducing the Phase G gap.
src-tauri/fixtures/** text eol=lf

# V42 Phase E: `src/lib/settings/generated/` is written by `settings::codegen`
# and re-generated by CI, which then runs `git diff --exit-code` over it. Byte-
# exactness is the whole check, so the bytes a Windows contributor commits have
# to be the bytes a Linux runner regenerates: no eol translation in either
# direction. (ts-rs and the defaults writer both emit LF.)
src/lib/settings/generated/** -text

# V42 F6 (#131): `src/lib/generated/` is written by `service::codegen` (the
# Tauri event names and the Tools tab reference lists) and diffed by the same
# CI step as the directory above, for the same reason and with the same rule.
src/lib/generated/** -text
